US investigates Chinese car, raising national data security risks
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The United States is investigating whether Chinese car imports pose a national security risk and could impose austerity measures over concerns over its car technology, the White House said on Thursday. Connect key technologies.
According to the White House Reuters, the Commerce Department's investigation is necessary because vehicles "collect sensitive data from drivers and their passengers (and) use cameras and sensors. "They regularly record details of US infrastructure."
Since vehicles can be "remotely controlled or disabled," the investigation will also look at self-driving vehicles.
"China's policy could flood our markets with its vehicles, posing a threat to our national security," US President Joe Biden said in a statement.
"I will not allow this to happen in my presence," he added.
White House officials told reporters it was too early to say what action would be taken and said no decision had been made on any possible ban or restriction on Chinese vehicles.
U.S. officials have told reporters that the US government has broad legal powers and could take any major action.
Biden called the effort an "unprecedented move" to ensure that US road vehicles imported from troubled countries such as China do not compromise its national security. We. According to Reuters, the WTO has agreed to renew the e-commerce agreement.
The World Trade Organization (WTO) has agreed to extend its e-commerce agreement for another two years after lengthy negotiations in Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates, as it failed to agree on a controversial trade deal. Others include the suppression of subsidies in agriculture and fisheries.
As the talks ended at midnight after a 16-hour process on Friday, the last-minute agreement to maintain e-commerce practices until 2026 was the most significant breakthrough.
The deal also came as a shock to some observers, with EU officials saying a week before it had reached a complete failure. According to Bloomberg News.
