Indonesia rice yield may fall more than 17% in January-April harvest
JAKARTA: Rice yields in the January-April harvest are expected to fall 17.52% from a year earlier due to dry weather, data from the Indonesian Bureau of Statistics showed on Friday.
The Southeast Asian country will produce 31.1 million tonnes of rice by 2023, according to Reuters, citing statistics released by the Bureau of Statistics. 10.1 million tons, down from 12.98 million last year.
Lower production expectations pushed retail prices for rice higher, pushing the Southeast Asian nation's February inflation higher than expected. The Bureau of Statistics' retail rice price index, which covers all grains, was 15,157 rupees ($ 0.9663) a kilogram in February, the highest ever. According to Reuters.
