US wants G-7 to speed up plans to use Russian assets for Ukraine
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - US President Joe Biden wants the Group of Seven (G-7) nations to make progress on a plan to use Russia's frozen state assets to support Ukraine as its leaders meet on Friday. June, according to people who know about this issue.
G-7 officials have discussed options for using Russia's central bank worth $ 280 billion, including the use of the money, Bloomberg reported, citing unnamed sources. As collateral for the issuance of bonds or the issuance of guarantees for the use of those frozen funds.
The debate comes as Ukraine faces a shortage of artillery and other ammunition as allies struggle to get supplies fast and $ 60 billion in economic aid is still stuck in the US Congress. Still.
According to sources familiar with the recent talks, Biden also privately told the Allies that if Ukraine loses the war, he believes international order will be in turmoil for at least the next five decades.
Allies agree that these funds should not be under Russia's control unless it promises to help rebuild Ukraine, but allies remain divided over whether to have a law. Confiscate all those assets or yana. The G-7 leaders are scheduled to meet in Italy in June this year.
The source told Bloomberg that Biden wanted to see the frozen assets used to help fund Ukraine's budget and later to rebuild Ukraine.
