The plan to convert 100,000 hectares of land into sustainable agricultural land comes as the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, together with the Cambodian Rice Federation and development partners, have officially launched the "Sustainable Rice Standards in Cambodia" (SRP) program. On February 27, 2024, which is an important program to promote rice production to a more sustainable standard and Cambodia's exports to international markets, especially the European market.
Duke Song Saran said that the project will be implemented from 2024 to 2030, he has planned to cooperate with 50,000 farmers on 100,000 hectares. The project will be implemented first in Battambang, then in Preah Vihear, Kampong Thom, Banteay Meanchey, Mondulkiri, Kampong Speu, Kampong Cham, Kampot and Kampong Chhnang.
Yang Saing Koma, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Agriculture, said that the conversion of 100,000 hectares of land into sustainable agricultural land or rice fields that can withstand climate change, called low-carbon rice fields, is a good direction to meet the plan. Set for 2030 to neutralize carbon.
Chan Pich, general manager of Signetchor FC Asia, a European rice exporter, said that the Sustainable Rice Production Standard, or SRP, appeared to be relatively new in the internationally recognized standard, but that it was not. Supported by the United Nations, this standardized rice production contributes to climate change mitigation, food safety and the environment.
He said that rice produced according to this standard may be more expensive than regular rice.
