MOSCOW (Reuters) - Russian leader Vladimir Putin has warned of the consequences of a nuclear war if the West sends troops to Ukraine. The warning was made on February 29 in an annual speech in Moscow. Putin says Russia has its weapons capable of firing on Western targets.
Russian President Vladimir Putin has warned of "tragedy" over any Western decision to send troops to Ukraine. The Russian leader's warning came in his annual address to the Russian nation on February 29, accusing the West of trying to lure Russia into an arms race.
"The consequences of such a decision will be tragic," the Russian president warned. In his annual address, President Putin accused the West of trying to lure Russia into an arms race.
The war in Ukraine, which began on February 24, 2022, has forced the formerly neutral country to change its position in search of self-defense. Sweden and Finland have announced their intention to join NATO in May 2022.
Finland later became a member of NATO in April 2023, and Sweden is expected to join NATO in the near future, after Turkey and Hungary, which once opposed their candidacy. Sweden has just ratified by its respective parliaments in early 2024.
Vladimir Putin has told Russians that Russia needs to strengthen its defenses along its borders with the West now that Sweden and Finland have joined NATO.
"We need to strengthen our forces in the direction of the West's strategy of neutralizing the threat as NATO expands the other two members, Sweden and Finland, into the alliance," the Russian leader said.
"They are talking about the possibility of sending NATO troops to Ukraine," Putin said. "But we also remember the fate of those who sent their party to our country in the past, and the consequences for those who want to interfere in the same thing will be even more tragic."
Russia has said it wants to expand NATO infrastructure to use Ukraine as a military base. In his 2024 speech in Moscow, Putin called on the West to "provoke a conflict in Ukraine" and that the West "continued to lie shamelessly by saying that Russia intends to attack Europe." ".
Many NATO countries, including the United States, Germany and the United Kingdom, have rejected France's call for the deployment of NATO troops to Ukraine.
The French leader's remarks come as the fighting situation in Ukraine enters its third year, seen as worsening due to a lack of military and military resources, internal discord is a major factor. At the same time, the decision of the Ukrainian leader to remove the old commander-in-chief and replace him with a new commander comes at a time when the war is raging.
For the North Atlantic military alliance, the idea of sending troops was banned, especially as NATO tried to avoid being dragged into a larger war with nuclear-armed Russia. But nothing prevents NATO members from participating in such activities individually or as a group, and NATO itself can only participate if all 31 of its members agree.
It is not possible to make any conclusions at this time, but at the same time, it is necessary to look at the strength of both sides, which side has what strength? According to documentary reports, NATO forces appear to be superior to Russian forces in terms of infantry. NATO is reported to have a total of nearly 6 million troops, compared to Russia's more than one million, which is nearly six.
Russia has the largest number of nuclear warheads in the world, which could put the world at risk. So even if NATO has some advantage over some Russian military equipment, it will not be able to make Russia with nuclear weapons surrender to war with each other.
