WASHINGTON (Reuters) - President Joe Biden's administration on Wednesday issued an executive order aimed at protecting US personal data by restricting data transfers to China, Russia and other countries, citing national security concerns. According to Reuters.
Reuters reports, citing a group of US officials, that the executive order would prevent the transfer of large amounts of biometric data, biometric, health and financial information of Americans by data brokers and other companies. Further to some "countries of concern".
The officials said there would also be restrictions on the transfer of any data on US government personnel to other countries of concern, such as Iran, North Korea, Cuba and Venezuela.
"China and Russia are buying sensitive US personal data from data brokers" and using it "to engage in a number of inappropriate activities, including cyber-espionage," the official said. "Extortion activities."
They added: "Purchasing data through data brokers is currently legal in the United States. That reflects the vulnerabilities in our national security toolkit, ”he said.
The issuance of the executive order is Washington's latest attempt to stem the flow of US data to China, which has been embroiled in a protracted trade and technology war with the United States. Find drugs on civil servants, all negative results
