Prommin's remarks came after a seminar on raising awareness of OECD membership organized by the Council for Economic and Social Development on Friday. The Bangkok Post reports that Thailand has submitted a letter of intent to the OECD to become a member, fulfilling its ambition to become a strong market economy that can compete globally.
He said the move was the first of 10 steps on the to-do list before the application could be approved. Mr Prommin said the OECD membership reflected the adoption of the highest world standards by developed countries, which would help attract trade and investment to Thailand.
The OECD sets international standards in line with member states, including a stable economic system, democratic governance, the rule of law, a quality education system, environmental protection and quality of life.
Mr Prommin said becoming an OECD member required regulatory reforms to create a conducive environment for investment. For example, currently applying for a factory license in Thailand requires the approval of up to 19 government agencies. As a result, the process should be cut short.
"The duty of the state is to support and facilitate trade and investment," Prommin said. "Regulatory reform is the lowest cost option to attract investment."
The OECD was established in 1961 by developed countries. Fifty years later, its membership has expanded to include a wider geographical area, including Latin America, Asia and the Pacific. Currently, there are only two member countries representing Asia: Japan and South Korea. Indonesia is another ASEAN member in the process of applying for membership. According to the Bangkok Post.
