President of the Association of Mathematicians of Cambodia: Early mathematics education in primary school lays the foundation for lifelong learning
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Chan Rath, President of the Cambodian Mathematician Community and President of the New Generation Pedagogical Research Center of the National Institute of Education, on February 29, 2024, stated that mathematics education at the primary level is to establish Fundamentals for lifelong learning.
Dr. Chanrath made this statement at a national conference on how to teach mathematics at the primary level under the theme: Everyone knows numbers. The conference was held at the National Institute of Education on the morning of February 29, 2024.
The conference was held in the presence of Mr. Chan Sophea, Director of the Department of Primary Education and represented by Dr. Hang Chuon Naron, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Education, Youth and Sports, organized by the Department of Primary Education of the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports in collaboration with the New Generation Pedagogical Research Center.
"Early mathematics education in primary school lays the foundation for lifelong learning and success in further education and other professions at the tertiary level," Dr. Chanrath said.
The president of the Cambodian Association of Mathematicians added that from infancy to primary school, children develop basic math skills that are important for the development of their social and emotional thinking skills that everyone must know. Proficiency.
At the same time, Dr. Chanrath said that the National Conference on Mathematical Teaching Methods at the Primary Level under the theme: "Everyone knows numbers, brings together educators, researchers, policy makers and industry experts." From around the world to find out the best practices on math education.
According to the president of the Cambodian Association of Mathematicians, the program focuses on increasing participation in mathematical solutions that enable all students to participate through the use of digital tools.
Dr. Chanrath added that the program uses an international approach, with interesting lessons with animated characters, games and games to encourage students to learn mathematical rules and develop lifelong skills.
All lessons are designed to be energetic, incorporating game activities and many real-life examples to illustrate the relevance of mathematics in everyday life. This is the additional confirmation of Dr. Chan Roth.
