(Phnom Penh): One of the stories on display at the Museum of Economics and Currency "Preah Isanvarman" (US) is the sunrise coin, as well as coins distributed in Greco-Hindu society and coins from the Empire. The Romans, for example, had been circulating in Cambodian society since the time of the Mountain Kingdom or Funan, meaning that Cambodians had been spending coins since the beginning of their history.
The mountain kingdom was an important point in the trade route between China and India, through excavated evidence that many foreign coins were found in the former capitals of Okeo and Angkor Borei, such as sunrise coins, Greek-Hindu coins, and Roman coins. Original.
According to Bless Gillion, although coins were used during the Mountain Kingdom, they were not commonly used throughout the empire, mostly for large business dealings with foreign merchants, as a religious offering. In short: Funan society is familiar with coins, but its use is for big business and important objects.
