Amid fears that Rafah will become a haven in the run-up to Ramadan, the US president has voiced hope, saying a temporary ceasefire in Gaza will emerge on Early next week.
Phase 1: A 45-day ceasefire in which all Israeli women hostages and men under the age of 19, the elderly and the sick will be exchanged with Palestinian women and children Detained in Israeli prisons. Israeli forces will withdraw from populated areas of Gaza and begin rebuilding hospitals and refugee camps.
Phase 2: The remaining Israeli hostages will be exchanged with Palestinian prisoners and Israeli forces will leave Gaza altogether.
Stage 3: Both sides will exchange ashes and corpses with each other. The agreement calls for increased aid and food aid for Gaza, and by the end of the 135-day ceasefire, Hamas has said it wants the war to end. Also.
The Israeli prime minister, who rejected the terms of the agreement, has vowed to continue planning attacks on Hamas. Israel is planning to evacuate people from Rafah, which Israel considers one of the last strongholds of Hamas, and Netanyahu has warned that a complete victory could be achieved for Israel in the coming months. This.
But despite disagreements, mediators are still negotiating for a ceasefire as the death toll and injuries rise, not just because of the airstrikes. Lack of equipment and medicine were reported as refugees were starving and they were surrounded by infectious diseases.
US officials have said they hope the weeks-long ceasefire will pave the way for an end to the war, but Prime Minister Netanyahu has said that after the ceasefire is over, attacks will resume. The three goals that Israel set before the end of the war.
