(Malaysia): At a bilateral meeting on the occasion of an official visit to Malaysia, Cambodian Prime Minister Hun Manet and his Malaysian counterpart Anwar Ibrahim agreed on the importance of trade and investment between the two countries. Both to boost the economy.
Hun Manet noted that bilateral trade between Cambodia and Malaysia is on the rise, but many potential sectors have yet to be used to strengthen economic partnership. In this regard, the President asked Malaysia to consider setting up a joint trade committee to promote trade.
Mr Anwar said Cambodia and Malaysia had high potential for the palm oil and rice trade. He stressed that the Ministry of Commerce of Cambodia and Malaysia will set up a forum to address challenges and find solutions to improve trade and tourism.
Anwar also saw the role of the two countries' customs authorities in facilitating the exchange of goods. According to Anwar, the two countries 'customs authorities have begun discussions on a memorandum of understanding between the two countries' customs authorities and are expected to conclude it soon.
In terms of investment, Anwar noted the rapid pace of development in food security, research, cooperation, investment, the halal food industry, renewable energy and high-tech industries, including EV and AI. Anwar expects more investment in Cambodia and from Cambodia to Malaysia.
To boost investment and trade between the two countries, Hun Manet and Anwar saw the important role of the private sector.
Regarding trade and investment, Mr. Anwar also welcomed the signing of a Memorandum of Understanding on "Cooperation on Promoting Financial Innovation and Payment System" between the National Bank of Cambodia and the Central Bank of Malaysia. Mr Anwar confirmed that Malaysia had already done so with Thailand and China. For Anwar, this cooperation will reduce the burden of relying solely on the US dollar.
