General Jones has become an environmental advocate for the private sector on national security and foreign policy issues, encourages increased inter-institutional cooperation, and is committed to maintaining the United States' global leadership for a century. Go with the current challenges.
General Jones arrived in Cambodia on February 24, 2024, bringing with him extensive experience in foreign policy, strategic planning, national security and military strategy.
During his visit to Cambodia, Mr. Jones will lead his team to pay a courtesy call and discuss with Cambodian Prime Minister Hun Manet, Deputy Prime Minister Tea Seiha, Minister of National Defense, and Deputy Prime Minister Sun Chanthol. First, the Council for the Development of Cambodia will meet with General Vong Pisen, Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces.
General Jones said he was ready to help boost trade and investment between Cambodia and the United States. The general believes that prioritizing increased trade between the United States and Cambodia is an opportunity to strengthen bilateral ties.
"Increasing trade with the United States could boost Cambodia's economy with great energy, employment opportunities and economic diversification, as well as support Cambodia's journey towards achieving status," Jones said. A high-income country.
Jones also saw that fruitful trade relations not only benefit Cambodia, but also the United States. It will help create new jobs for American products and services, as well as open the door for American businesses to invest and expand their operations in Cambodia.
The discussion also provided an opportunity for major business people attending the event to voice their views as elements for better trade and investment between Cambodia and the United States.
