Japanese Government Provides Nearly $ 770,000 Grant for the Promotion of Environmental Education, Improving Early Childhood Education and Improving the Medical Cooperation System in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): The Government of Japan, through the Embassy of Cambodia, has signed a total grant of US $ 768,627 to three recipient units under a grant scheme for a Japanese NGO project.
The Grant Agreement Signing Ceremony was signed on February 26, 2024 by the Acting Chargé d'Affaires of the Embassy of Japan in Cambodia, Mr. Taninai Eichitomu, and the representatives of the three recipients for the following projects:
In this project, Nature Center Risen will take action to promote environmental education from childhood in some kindergartens in Sihanoukville to address increasingly difficult issues along with economic growth. The project aims to promote living with nature for children and their parents.
Second: Improving the accessibility and quality of early childhood education of public kindergartens in Oddar Meanchey province (US $ 414,756) Recipient Institution: Volunteer Peace Organization (SVA).
In this project, the Volunteer Organization will improve the educational environment, both in terms of individuals and facilities that support young children, to address the education gap between urban and rural areas. In addition, the volunteer organization aims to improve access and quality of activities in public kindergartens in Oddar Meanchey province, located in northwestern Cambodia.
Third: Project on Improving the Medical Cooperation System in the Northeastern Region of Cambodia through Strengthening the Practice of Surgery (US $ 193,731) Recipient Institution: International Development and Relief Fund (FIDR).
The Japanese government has been providing grants to Japanese non-governmental organizations (NGOs) since 2002 to support their economic and social development projects implemented in developing countries or regions, according to Tani Naiichi Tomu. He said that in the last 20 years, the Embassy of Japan has provided assistance to more than 150 projects in Cambodia through this program. These projects have directly contributed to improving the quality of life of the Cambodian people.
"With the financial support of the government, we will strive to leverage Japan's long-standing experience and environmental education knowledge for the promotion of environmental education in collaboration with a team of "The Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports of the Kingdom of Cambodia to reclaim the beautiful sea and city of Sihanoukville and protect the lives of boys and girls, which is a precious asset of Cambodia."
Mr. Sakeki Kazato, Country Director of the Foundation for Development and Relief International, said that since 1996, FIDR has been working with the National Children's Hospital in Phnom Penh for 20 years to develop a treatment system. National level children have achieved many achievements in medical development at the National Children's Hospital, such as the operating theater and the hospital.
