Ministry of Foreign Affairs: Malaysian authorities take action against those who plan to protest against the President in accordance with ASEAN principles
(Phnom Penh): The spokesman of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Cambodia on February 26, 2024 issued three explanations in response to a reporter's question about the exercise of the right of expression of some Cambodians during the official visit of His Excellency Hun Manet, Prime Minister of the Kingdom of Cambodia in Malaysia.
The clarification came after news that Malaysian authorities had warned of action against Cambodian opposition groups in their country in connection with an attempt to demonstrate against President Hun Manet.
In this statement, a spokesman for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs stated that as a principle of democracy, the right to freedom of expression must be within the framework of the rule of law. In the past, there have been many comments and interpretations about the right to freedom of expression during the Prime Minister's official visit to Malaysia, but there has been no mention of laws and principles. A spokesman for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs confirmed the following:
1. "Non-interference in the internal affairs of ASEAN Member States" is one of the principles set out in Chapter 1, Article 2 of the ASEAN Charter. Cambodia and Malaysia not only have good friendly relations and cooperation, but also ASEAN member countries that must fully respect the ASEAN Charter.
2. In principle, in international practice, the host country has an obligation to ensure the dignity, security, safety and smoothness of the visits / invitations of high-ranking state dignitaries. The actions of the Malaysian authorities are aimed at ensuring proper adherence to the principles outlined in the first and second points.
3. Foreigners, including diplomats with immunity, have an obligation to abide by the laws of the country in which they live. Therefore, the exercise of the right to express one's opinion is in accordance with the laws of Malaysia.
