Sub-regions in Asia, which are heavily involved in the export of high-carbon products to Europe, especially Central and Western Asia, will be more adversely affected by CBAM and its emissions-related trading systems. By the European Union.
The ADB report said that given the expected impact, particularly on developing countries in Asia, appropriate incentive mechanisms are needed to encourage broad adoption of carbon pricing.
The report also recommends measures to reduce carbon emissions in international trade processes and global value chains. Carbon emissions from these sources are growing faster than any other source, and they are growing faster in Asia.
Among the recommendations are the implementation of policies focused on encouraging trade in climate-friendly products and services. Supporting environmental regulations and standards, facilitating the transfer of green technology, and supporting governments and international institutions in promoting green infrastructure and investment.
The report further calls for global cooperation to establish a universally acceptable accountability framework to effectively monitor emissions embedded in products and services.
Among other key findings, AEIR 2024 also shows that despite concerns about the risk of global diversity, global value chains in Asia have recovered after the Kovid 19 pandemic.
The Asian Development Bank (ADB) is committed to achieving environmental prosperity, resilience and sustainability in the Asia-Pacific region in line with its efforts to eradicate extreme poverty. Founded in 1966, the bank is governed by 68 member countries, including 49 countries from Asia and the Pacific. Battambang Provincial Administration Celebrates Winners of Public Service Units: Model in Education and Health 2023
