South Korean government urges medical staff to return to work as strike enters second week
SEOUL (Reuters) - The South Korean government on Monday urged most junior doctors, mostly trainees, to return to work and warned that they could face legal action if they remain stubborn and do not return to the hospital. In the next three days. This was released by the AFP news agency on Monday, February 26, 2024.
Official figures show that a total of more than 10,000 junior doctors, or 80.5 percent of all medical practitioners in South Korea, have resigned as part of a campaign against the government's plans. The goal is to dramatically increase the number of students admitted to medical schools in the face of a shortage of doctors and the rapid growth of the elderly population in this East Asian country.
As of Monday, a total of 9,006 medical staff had not been present at the workplace, and the South Korean government had warned that legal action would be taken, including arrests, arrests and suspensions of doctors. Strikes are inevitable. The South Korean Ministry of Health has set a deadline of February 29 for them to return to work.
