The following is a statement from the United States and China: Finding new options to reduce the debt of developing countries.
WASHINGTON: The United States and China are discussing new measures to curb the wave of failed governments' debt repayments, one of the most important attempts in years of economic cooperation between the two powers. Two. According to Bloomberg News.
The talks, citing sources familiar with the matter, said discussions of measures, including extending the loan period before countries failed to pay their debts, were aimed at easing the annual debt service burden of $ 400,000. Millions of dollars for poor countries and finding alternatives to the high loan rates that those countries are facing in the market.
In addition to the repayment delays, other ideas being discussed include increasing funding from the World Bank and other multilateral banks. An important point is to put those measures in place before countries fail to repay their debts and include them in formal debt restructuring talks with creditors.
Any joint proposal on global government debt between Washington and Beijing that emerges from this discussion is likely to need the support of the G-20, the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank, as all parties try to resolve the debt issue. Global since the outbreak.
And ultimately, the proposal will also require the broad involvement of private creditors, who make up the bulk of government lending in developing countries, and expect a stronger voice at the negotiating table.
The Treasury Ministry declined to comment on the specifics of the talks, saying in a statement: "We often talk with China about government debt concerns. "And we talk to many countries about how to make sure the international financial framework is meeting the financing needs of low-income countries."
Chinese Foreign Ministry spokeswoman Mao Ning said her country "pays close attention to the debt problems of developing countries." She added at a regular press conference in Beijing on Friday that the United States and China discussed the issue "through multilateralism and bilateral networks."
