(Pursat): Pursat Provincial Administration and Provincial Chief on Saturday 15th and Sunday 1st of Roach Khemak, the year of Panchasak, BE 2567, which is on February 24-25, 2024, organized the Meak Bochea ceremony according to the traditional tradition. Buddhism to commemorate three major events: 1- The four-cornered assembly, 2-Celebrating the day on which he performed the Patimok advice, and 3-Celebrating the day on which the Buddha gave his life as a sangha.
The ceremony was held at Wat Keo Vichey in Kandeang commune, Kandeang district, Pursat province, presided over by Mr. Mak Ra, Chairman of the Provincial Council, Mr. Khoy Rida, Governor of Pursat Province, and invited and attended by officials. Monks, monks, ladies and gentlemen, members of provincial councils, provincial governors, department heads, units, civil servants, the armed forces, students, teachers and Buddhists, more than 1,000 people.
Preah Brahmaphirat Keo Kan, Honorary Royal Council of the Kingdom of Cambodia, Preah Por Vossa Vimol, Provincial Chief and Chief Prelate of Wat Keo Vichey Up in order to commemorate three major events.
Meak Bochea is considered to be a big religious festival and is always held in pagodas every year, never missing in the sense of participating in the maintenance and adherence to the traditions and pure spirit in the field of Buddhism, unity and cooperation. Celebrate Meak Bochea smoothly.
Recognizing the value of Meak Bochea, the monks said that all the pagodas celebrated this festival together to spread a broad understanding of what we have done for ourselves, our families and our families. Our community, therefore, as Buddhists, must participate in preserving this traditional religion and culture for the benefit of our descendants to see, know, understand and obey.
He hopes that monks, nuns everywhere, pagodas and all Buddhists will continue to strengthen, expand and maintain the traditional customs, respect the Buddha's advice by keeping the five precepts and the eight precepts is to seek the state of mourning without suffering. Having a mental problem means the same thing as peace, teaching Buddhists to strive for peace in society and the world.
