India continues to lift tariffs on black rice exports to control domestic prices
The 20% export tax, which expires on March 31, 2024, will be levied, Bloomberg reported, citing a statement from the Indian Ministry of Finance on Wednesday (February 21st). Renewal indefinitely.
With elections coming up in the coming months, the government of Prime Minister Narendra Modi is worried about raising food prices, which is half of India's consumer price package. Food inflation was 8.3% in January.
The renewal of tariffs on the world's second-largest rice-growing country will further tighten global supply and boost international rice prices. This result will affect some countries in West Africa and the Middle East, which depend on India for most of their staple food needs.
