Ms. Thorn Sopheak said, "As a young person, I would like to see more and more people involved in conserving endangered wildlife. I think if we do not conserve it together now, it will be less. "The next generation of Cambodians will never know him. If we start conserving from now on, the next generation will be able to know him."
She added that although we do not have much involvement in wildlife conservation and natural resources, we are an important part of working together to protect wildlife by inspiring animal love. Forests and natural resources, starting with one person.
According to Mr. Dim Kanan, a lecturer and researcher at the Faculty of Veterinary Medicine of the Royal University of Agriculture, he had visited the Cardamom Mountains and was very attracted to the beauty of natural resources. Given the opportunity for him as well as young people to learn more about natural resources, forests, the importance of wildlife, biodiversity that helps prevent climate change.
Mr. Dim Kanan continued that the organization of the exhibition "Forever Cardamom Mountain" is very important, which is a reminder to students, youth and people to understand, especially not to forget the importance of life. Diversified natural resources.
The Cardamom Mountains are considered to be home to more than 50 endangered and globally endangered species: Asian elephants, tigers, mountain crocodiles, big bears, small bears, crowned turtles, Greek turtles. Peacocks and other rare species of trees as an important source of water and rainwater for the sustainability of the Tonle Sap.
Most of the cardamom forest cover is covered by dense, semi-dense forest, which is an important source for carbon storage and is a mangrove forest that is conducive to rare species to value the conservation and development of the national economy through development. Energy sector, carbon credit sales and ecotourism.
The Cardamom Mountains are also the most important biodiversity hotspots, especially the last remaining elephant corridor in Asia and home to 52 endangered species and are on the Union Red List. International for the Conservation of Natural Resources (IUCN).
National Assembly President Khuon Sodary Presides Over the Meak Bochea Ceremony at Preah Moha Sak Moni Chet, Phnom Preah Reach Trop, Ponhea Leu District, Kandal Province
(Phnom Penh): National Assembly President Samdech Khuon Sodary, President of the National Assembly, presided over the Meak Bochea ceremony held at Preah Moha Sak Moni Chet, Phnom Preah Reach Trop, Ponhea Leu District, Kandal Province on Saturday morning. 2567 is on February 24, 2024.
