(Phnom Penh): The Cambodian Human Rights Committee held a press release on the outcome of the first meeting of the National Report of Cambodia and the implementation of the International Convention on the Protection of All Persons from Forced Disappearance at its 26th session. Charged with the disappearance of the United Nations on 19-20 February 2024 in Palais Wilson, Geneva, Switzerland.
The Cambodian delegation was led by Mr. Keo Sothy, Vice Chairman of the Cambodian Human Rights Committee, and consisted of Mr. Nak Ser Kirin, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Justice, Lt. Gen. Ey Sok Khi, Deputy Commissioner General of the National Police, Ministry of Interior, and Ms. Long Sonita, and Mr. Pa Ponnarada, Representative of the Human Rights Committee. Cambodia.
The National Report Review Meeting is a regular mechanism of the Committee on Forced Disappearances conducted to monitor the implementation of the provisions of the Convention by States Parties.
During the two-day dialogue, members of the Committee on Forced Disappearances praised Cambodia's efforts to submit its first national report, explaining the basis of national laws, regulations and legal documents. Which ensures compliance with the provisions of this Convention.
At the same time, the Committee also raised questions for information, clarification, interpretation and further explanations related to areas such as the national legal framework, the establishment of national human rights institutions, anti-corruption, state law on emergency workers, migration, anti-corruption. Human trafficking, independence, the judiciary, the juvenile justice system, and so on.
The Committee on Forced Disappearances commended Cambodia for its efforts and acknowledged Cambodia's willingness and commitment to implement the Convention, as well as for its well-explained and well-responsive dialogue. Build on Cambodia's first national report on the implementation of the International Convention for the Protection of All Persons from Enforced Disappearance.
In addition, the Committee on Forced Disappearances is ready to provide technical assistance to Cambodia in order to facilitate the better implementation of this International Convention.
This morning, Samdech Thipdey Hun Manet announced the winners of the Model Public Service Unit in Education and Health in 2023 and announced the implementation for 2024
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of Thursday, February 13, 2024, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Moha Bovathibodi Hun Manet, presided over the announcement of the winners of the Model Public Service Unit in the field. Education and Health 2023 and announced for implementation in 2024.
This initiative was implemented through Sub-Decree No. 54. Dated April 30, 2018 on the evaluation and recognition of model public service units in the field of education and health.
Based on this spirit, the Royal Government in the 7th legislature of the National Assembly under the leadership of Samdech Moha Bovathibodi Hun Manet continues to pay more attention and encourage the movement of the competition through the evaluation and recognition of model public service units in the field. Education and health sector, which was implemented by Samdech Akka Moha Sena Padei Techo Hun Sen in 2018.
The competition of local public service providers will continue for kindergartens, primary schools, secondary schools, high schools, health centers, referral hospitals, complete set of activities (Level 1) (CPA2), referral hospitals provide set of activities, complete level 2 (CPA2). ) And Referral Hospitals provide Level 3 Completion Activity Services (CPA3).
+ Implementation, evaluation and recognition of model public service providers
In order to ensure the accuracy and participation of all levels, the evaluation and recognition of the sample public service providers was organized into three levels before requesting recognition from the Royal Government as a model entity, which includes the evaluation by the municipal evaluation committee / District / Khan Capital / Provincial Evaluation Committee and National Evaluation Committee (CEC)
The evaluation at the national level is divided into three stages, including: (1) the preliminary stage, (2) the selection stage, the winners and (3) the final evaluation stage.
The National Evaluation Commission sets out sub-criteria and detailed criteria for judging each of the key criteria, and can also be used as an orientation map to improve the management and service delivery of schools and hospitals.
Later, CJV Decided to continue to carry out assessments at the secondary and primary schools for education and health centers at the health level, but the official implementation of the inter-ministerial prakas has been delayed until 2022 due to the spread of Kovid. -19.
In the spirit of continuing job sustainability, the Ministry of Public Works and the Ministry of Public Works and Transport (MPWT) has promoted the implementation of the evaluation and recognition of the model in the education and health sectors (2022 and 2023) with a total of candidates in secondary education. 42 from 22 provincial capitals, 46 primary schools from 24 provincial capitals, and in the health sector, there are 43 health center candidates from 22 provincial capitals.
