President of the Education Services Federation of Cambodia, Kong Samneang, considered the 9th National Reading Day a special event. Especially in the fact that the 9th National Reading Day on the birthday of Samdech Sang Chuon Nath is a reminder to a scholar in Cambodia. He added that at the same time, it is to cultivate reading habits and reading ability of children after a few years off.
The President of the Federation of Education Services in Cambodia will also announce to members to celebrate Reading Day on the day set by the Ministry of Education. He hopes that this day will help children, especially students, to be able to read better.
The CEO of Wesleyan Education Group stated, "We lack a lot of our Khmer books, so when it comes to reading, there are more writers." Reading also creates thinking because the source of knowledge is in this reading and, as the saying goes, people will be positively affected by two things: first, the good people we meet and second, good books. One that we read.
In 2023, the Ministry of Education stated that worldwide, about 30 to 40 percent of children going to school are illiterate and illiterate, which affects the quality of education. This figure means that not only in Cambodia, there are about 500 million students in the world who go to school and are illiterate.
Mr. Pech Bolen observed that private and public schools do not have much training in reading Khmer literature. He thinks that National Reading Day is not just a day that must be attended annually, but to make reading a habit.
Regarding the strategy to promote reading, this expert said:
At the same time, Mr. Kong Samneang found that around the world, children who go to school are illiterate and can not write properly, about 30 to 40 percent due to the protracted 19 problems. He said that because children before he reaches the first grade, they have to go through kindergarten, including kindergarten level 1, kindergarten level 2 and kindergarten level 3.
Mr. Kong Samneang continued, "In the first grade, if the child has a foundation in reading and an alphabet, then he will be able to grasp this level better." In particular, the Kovid phase starts from 2019 to the end of 2021, the beginning of 2022, then we rest. "We think this period is really impactful and it is a concern for parents and for the school as a whole."
According to the president of the Education Services Federation in Cambodia, learning online has both advantages and disadvantages. Good results, although not very good, but at least the children know some letters. But as a child, he loves the phone and uses it a lot.
"Even at the end of the Kovid stage, he told his parents on condition that he do this and that until he asked for a phone call," he said. "Only if he looks at the phone is it possible. In fact, it is very annoying for children who use the phone."
As a strategy to help reading, Mr. Kong Samneang said that the members of the Federation of Cambodian Education Services are working in private schools to focus on children in the first and second grade on reading words, because the members of the Federation also have negative effects related to reading. Read. So the school encourages reading and reading programs.
The school cooperates with parents by keeping children close to the phone at home, keeping them close to books and carrying lots of books. At the same time, when traveling, we encourage parents to encourage their children to read.
