(Phnom Penh): Raising chickens as a family is very important to provide income and nutrition to rural families in Cambodia.
A major challenge for local poultry farmers is the high mortality rate, especially in the early stages of production. This low productivity affects the livelihood of the farmer raising chickens as a family and limits his access to protein.
In 2022, Australia will fund a research project through the Australian Center for International Agricultural Research (ACIAR) to increase productivity and local climate resilience on local poultry, Skouy, by providing technical and technical support. Innovative technology for chicken farmers in Cambodia.
The project worked with farmers for the purpose of developing chicken breeds. Participating farmers were trained in hen management techniques, biosecurity measures, artificial insemination techniques and data recording in breeding farms.
Within a year, the project successfully developed the first generation of squirrels. This breed has been improved to increase productivity, resilience to increase chicken production for farmers. The price of skim chicken is currently $ 5 per kilogram, while its eggs are $ 0.3. The main breed of the Skuy breed is sold for about $ 15 per head.
Dr. Tum Sothira, Director of the National Institute for Animal Health and Production Research, who is leading the project in Cambodia, said: "High and high egg production), especially resistant to local climate), especially to meet the growing demand of domestic chickens and domestic breeds, which contribute to increasing farmers' incomes."
"In the upcoming project cycle, I would like to see the project expand to other areas so that there will be farmers raising chickens with good local breeds suitable for both family and commercial production systems," he added.
He emphasized on meeting the technical requirements of local partner institutions, especially the knowledge and skills needed for poultry farmers to continue to operate independently after the project is completed.
