SHANGHAI: New Year's holiday spending in China last week rose above pre-epidemic levels, a good sign for China, which is struggling with a slump, official data show. Consumption costs and deflation.
Domestic spending on entertainment, food and travel has risen during the New Year holiday this year, AFP reported, citing a statement issued by the Chinese Ministry of Culture and Tourism on Sunday. Officially ends on Saturday.
The ministry said 474 million Chinese travelers traveled the country during the eight-day holiday, up 19 percent from 2019, the world's largest annual trip. .
This year is the second New Year holiday in China after the abrupt cancellation of the end of 2022 due to the Kuwait-Sunni policy to tighten the prevention of epidemics in China, which has reduced consumer spending and Affect business confidence through blockades and long-term deregulation.
