Indonesia and Singapore are working towards cooperation on carbon capture and storage
JAKARTA: Singapore has become the first country to sign a Memorandum of Understanding (LOI) with Indonesia on cross-border cooperation on carbon sequestration and storage (CCS), the government said on Thursday. ). According to Reuters.
Indonesia, which sees potential to develop the natural gas sector and CCS as part of a global shift towards green fuel, last month issued a presidential regulation allowing CCS operators to split 30, Reuters reported. Percentage of their carbon storage capacity for imported carbon dioxide (CO2) (or carbon dioxide).
Singapore, which aims to have zero emissions by 2050, is looking at low-carbon technologies such as hydrogen and CCS as part of its carbon offsets.
According to a joint statement issued on Thursday by the two countries, Singapore and Indonesia will set up a working group to explore a bilateral agreement that binds legal obligations on transboundary CO2 transport and This gas storage between the two neighboring countries.
Jodi Mahardi, Indonesia's deputy co-ordinator for energy management, told Reuters that the draft agreement marked "a milestone in our efforts towards sustainable development and environmental management."
According to Reuters, the International Energy Agency will use its global carbon consumption and storage capacity to increase its capacity to about 1 giga per year (gtpa) by 2030 and 6 gtpa by 2050 to achieve Zero emissions by 2050. About 40 million tons of carbon dioxide will be captured and stored worldwide by 2022.
Critics say CCS is an expensive technology and has not yet been proven effective, but Indonesia intends to become a regional CO2 storage hub. Cambodia for February 18, 2024
