(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Information on February 17, 2024 issued a press release on "Activities to Celebrate World Radio Day 2024", the core and essential role of traditional media in the digital age in maintaining peace and Develop the nation sustainably and environmentally. This is according to a press release obtained by Fresh News.
According to the press release, the Ministry of Information's Radio Week campaign was successfully concluded on 16 February 2024 with the participation of dignitaries, leaders and civil servants of the Ministry of Information, as well as students from universities. A total of 221 media outlets to celebrate World Radio Day 2024.
Under the leadership of Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Minister of Information, the Radio Week campaign was organized with the aim of disseminating to the public in Cambodia and around the world more about the important role and values of the radio, especially the National Radio of Cambodia. As a traditional media for more than four decades, it has actively contributed to building, maintaining peace and developing the country to be as prosperous and free as it is today.
The Radio Week campaign, which took place from 13 to 16 February 2024, focused on a number of key activities to highlight the importance of radio, which is always close to the people and closely related to life. Daily for people of all ages and walks of life in providing information, knowledge, ideas, life skills and entertainment, education and rich information in all forms and everywhere without limits.
At the same time, the Minister also commented on the evolution of the media in Cambodia, the current media situation with the constant advancement of technology and the trend of people access to information, which provides new opportunities. And some challenges to the information and audio sector.
However, the Ministry of Information and Information Operators, through the celebration of this year's World Radio Day, has once again demonstrated its commitment to enhancing the role of all traditional radio and media outlets as connected information agencies. Communicating education, knowledge and entertainment to contribute to cultivating national consciousness and pride, cultivating a love of culture and tradition, as well as maintaining peace and national identity.
"At all costs, radio and other traditional media must continue to live in new contexts and complement each other with modern media," he said. "The Ministry of Information will continue to preserve and develop traditional media for further development through continued integration and reform of broadcast programs in line with social developments."
At the same time, all students also learned about the presence of the National Radio in broadcasting in all parts of the Kingdom of Cambodia, both AM and FM radio, network radio and in the provinces, as well as aspects of the technical work of broadcasting radio programs. .
During the visit, all students learned about the functions and daily work of various departments, including the Technical Department, the Department of Foreign Broadcasting, the Department of Local and International Information, and the Program Department, as well as visiting the loudspeakers.
