Israel raids Gaza's largest hospital as concerns over Rafah attack rise
Gaza: Israel launched a new offensive in the southern Gaza Strip on Thursday (February 15), with the military also raiding the region's largest hospital. Posted online, showing the chaos, the screams and the sound of gunfire in the dark corridors filled with dust and smoke. This is according to the release by Reuters on February 15, 2024.
Confirming that its special forces were conducting a military operation at the Al-Nasser Hospital in Khan Younis, the Israeli Armed Forces said it had received "reliable information" that the bodies of the hostages had been captured. October 7 may be in place. But a Hamas spokesman denied the allegations, calling them "fabricated."
Health authorities in Hamas-controlled areas say Israel has forced people to evacuate and forced the families of hospital staff to leave.
As Israeli bombings devastated many neighborhoods and forced most people out of their homes, hospitals became a major destination for refugees. Likely safe.
Israel has accused Hamas of using hospitals, ambulances and other medical equipment for military purposes and released videos of its troops finding weapons in tunnels and under hospitals.
The Hamas-controlled health ministry said 107 people, mostly women and children, had been killed in the attack.
The United Nations Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs (OCHA) said Wednesday that Al-Nasser Hospital was under siege by Israeli forces, who allegedly opened fire on the building, endangering the lives of medical staff. Illness will force thousands to leave.
The fighting at the hospital comes as Israel faces increasing international pressure to show restraint in its war in Gaza after vowing to launch an offensive into Rafah, a safe haven. The latter for civilians. Image from Reuters Thailand announces additional holidays for New Year to boost tourism
BANGKOK: Thailand's cabinet has approved an additional holiday for civil servants during the traditional New Year celebrations in April as the Southeast Asian nation seeks to sustain a revitalized tourism industry. . According to Bloomberg News.
The April 12 holiday will apply to government offices and other state agencies and will extend the Sankranti holiday to five days, from April 12 to 16.
The move will boost tourism and domestic spending, Bloomberg News quoted Thai Prime Minister Setha Thavisin as saying after a cabinet meeting on Tuesday.
Since taking office six months ago, Setha's government has put in place measures to boost tourism, which he calls a "quick win", to boost the sector's average growth of less than 2% over a period of time. Decades ago. The sector contributes about 12% of GDP and nearly one-fifth of employment.
Thailand saw a 48% increase in foreign tourist arrivals to 4.4 million since the beginning of this year, compared with the same period last year, with more Chinese tourists visiting the country. Thailand under the Visa Waiver Program Official data show that nearly 200,000 travelers from mainland China flocked to Thailand last week during the Chinese New Year holiday, up 68 percent from the previous week.
The Thai government has set a target of 35 million foreign visitors by 2024, with 8 million expected from China. Overland tourists accounted for more than 25% of the total 40 million foreign tourists in 2019, but the number of Chinese tourists fell by only about 3.5 million out of 28 million foreign tourists. Who visited Thailand last year.
