Myanmar state media on Saturday (February 10) said that the Myanmar military regime has announced the implementation of military service on all young men and women as the country is increasingly struggling to fight the armed forces. Minority weapons in many parts of the country after the regime seized power from Aung San Suu Kyi's elected government in February 2021.
The Washington-based US Institute of Peace notes that 21,000 soldiers have been lost in casualties, defections and displacement since the military came to power. Only about 150,000.
Under the new law, all men between the ages of 18 and 35 and women between the ages of 18 and 27 must serve two years in the military, while specialists such as doctors over the age of 45 must serve. Military for 3 years. Not only that, this military duty can also be extended up to 5 years during the state of emergency.
Myanmar has been plagued by violence and unrest since the military staged a coup in 2021. But since October 2023, the Myanmar military has lost large numbers of troops in fighting with three ethnic armed alliances that have been uniting against the military regime. This is the biggest challenge the Myanmar military regime has been facing since it first came to power in 1962.
The law on military service in Myanmar was enacted in 2010. However, this law has not been implemented until now and those who evade or do not follow may face up to 5 years in prison. And donate blood to patients suffering (Video inside)
