The Ministry of Environment plans to install additional cameras in the Cardamom Mountains to monitor the presence of wildlife and inspect the food of big tigers.
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Environment plans to install additional cameras in the Cardamom Mountains to monitor the presence and absence of wildlife that feeds on big tigers such as deer and wild boar. This was stated by Dr. Eang Sophalleth, Minister of the Environment, in a recent interview with the media.
According to the minister, on the advice of technicians from India, one camera should be spaced one kilometer apart. During the three months between the dry season and the rainy season, the camera can capture images and experts can analyze the species as food for the restoration of big tigers.
It should be noted that on November 12, 2022, Cambodia and India signed a Memorandum of Understanding on Cooperation in Biodiversity Conservation, Sustainable Wildlife Management and Strategies for the Restoration of Big Tigers and their Habitats in Cambodia. Cambodia.
The Big Tiger Rehabilitation Project was established after disappearing from Cambodia in 2007, while around the world it is estimated that there were still about 3,200 big tigers in 2010. This number is down 95% from the number that was present in the early 20s, when there were about 100,000 in the world.
