(Phnom Penh): Mr. Kaing Tong Ngy, spokesman for the Cambodian Microfinance Association, said that the microfinance sector in Cambodia continues to maintain good stability, despite not much growth.
According to the report of the Cambodian Microfinance Association, by the end of 2023, the microfinance sector, excluding data from banking institutions, provided loans of $ 5.12 billion to 1.57 million clients.
Meanwhile, deposits at the four deposit-taking microfinance institutions (MDIs) totaled $ 2.2 billion from about 2.01 million depositors over the same period.
Speaking to Fresh News on February 13, 2024, Mr. Kaing Tong Ngy said that overall in 2023, the microfinance sector in Cambodia has not grown significantly in the last two quarters. There has been a slight decline in loan size and number of clients, but the financial sector has maintained good stability.
He said that the decline in credit volumes was normal due to the current slowdown in economic growth, so credit growth has slowed.
"However, if we look at the overall data, we can see that the microfinance sector in Cambodia still has an important and stable role, which will be able to continue to provide financial services to people across Cambodia, regardless of you," he said. There is no high or low income or middle income for financial services, including loans, deposits, other payments are tailored to the needs of the people of Cambodia.
The microfinance sector directly employs 21,567 Cambodians nationwide with a network of 904 offices.
