Donald Trump reiterates risks to NATO future
WASHINGTON: Donald Trump, who is running for president, has reaffirmed his position on US support for NATO members who have complied with the budget constraints set by the NATO Charter. . The statement made NATO Secretary-General Jens Stoltenberg very concerned about the future of NATO, which is committed to providing security for its members, especially Ukraine.
According to NATO figures for 2023, 19 of its 30 member states are spending less than 2% of their annual GDP on defense, including Germany, Norway and France. Well. But in most countries bordering Ukraine, Russia or neighboring Belarus, the cost goes beyond this guideline.
The NATO leader said Donald Trump's remarks "increased the risk for US and European troops." "I expect that no matter who wins the next presidential election, the United States will remain a NATO country committed to and defending the North Atlantic Alliance," he said.
Russia began declaring war on Ukraine on February 24, 2022. With only about 12 days left until Monday, February 12, 2022, it will reach its second anniversary and enter its third year. Donald Trump has been critical of Joe Biden's policy of providing military funding to Ukraine in the past.
The $ 60 billion bailout proposal, which was blocked at the end of 2023, was again proposed in early 2024, but was again blocked. Donald Trump on Saturday, February 10, 2024, during the election campaign, praised the blockade of aid to Ukraine, which he considered a disaster if the United States will Give to Ukraine, yes.
