Because it lasts up to 15 days, it is the longest festival on the Chinese calendar. The dates change from year to year, but usually between January 21 and February 20.
Chinese New Year is centuries old and very important. This festival is held to pay homage to angels and ancestors. It is held in some countries and territories where a large number of Chinese people live, including mainland China, Hong Kong, Macau, Taiwan, Singapore, Thailand, Cambodia, Indonesia, the Philippines, as well as in China Towns, which are located in some other countries in China. In the world.
The festival is considered an important holiday for the Chinese people and has influenced the culture of neighboring countries.
For Chinese people, New Year is a special occasion for families to get together to eat together, especially dinner. It is also an opportunity for Chinese people to clean their homes again to ward off bad things and evil spirits and accept good luck in the new year. The windows and doors are often decorated with red paper or two-line poems representing "good luck, happiness, wealth and longevity."
In addition to offerings, there are other activities that Chinese people usually do on New Year's Day, such as lighting fireworks, sharing barbecues or food with each other. "If people spend more or share more on New Year's Eve, it will bring more profits to their families," the Chinese said.
