The farewell ceremony for the Cambodian Blue Hat Army was held on the morning of February 08, 2024, presided over by General Sem Sovanny, First Vice President of the National Committee and Director General of the National Peacekeeping Center, and the leadership of the National Peacekeeping Center. Many more at Phnom Penh International Airport (VIP).
General Sem Sovanny stated that the mission in the Republic of Lebanon is the 15th year and that the 836th Multi-Skilled Engineering is the 14th Division, which has participated in carrying out fruitful tasks with proud results. On behalf of the Cambodian Blue Hat Army, we must maintain cleanliness, good discipline, and adhere strictly to the standards of the United Nations, with safety first and foremost, until we succeed without any members being injured or sacrificed for any reason. .
General Sem Sovanny reminded all forces to continue to unite as one and to consider safety first, especially during the performance of duties, do not underestimate any situation and must learn from experience and good lessons to correct all Mistakes made to achieve success for the motherland, family and yourself.
General Sem Sovanny said that for the troops participating in the mission area, it must be strictly enforced according to the directives of the commander of the UN force in the region, which must be implemented properly to ensure safety.
On this occasion, General Sem Sovanny conveyed greetings from Samdech Prime Minister Hun Manet, Prime Minister of Cambodia, General Tea Seiha, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of National Defense of Cambodia and Mr. Sok Chenda Sophea, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation and Chairman. The National Committee also assigned to the 836th Special Engineering Engineering Corps.
General Sem Sovanny said at the ceremony that the 836 Multidisciplinary Engineering has undergone UN-standard training, both general training and core training, to meet the specific needs of the mission area.
The general added that the unit would be led by a female commander in response to a UN strategy aimed at empowering women, both quantitatively and qualitatively, to join forces with donor countries in the mission area and Respond to the commitment of the leaders in the Royal Government of Cambodia to expand the participation of women to more than 20% in both the army and the officers, officers and military observers.
Over the past 18 years, Cambodia has sent a total of 9,465 troops, including 801 women, to 12 missions in 10 countries, including the Republic of Sudan, South Sudan, Chad, Lebanon, Cyprus, Syria, Central Africa, Mali Yemen. And the Democratic Republic of the Congo. Currently, the Cambodian force is carrying out 640 missions, including 119 women in five countries: South Sudan, Lebanon, Mali, Central Africa and the Democratic Republic of Congo.
