This Buddha statue is a unique artifact that represents the artistic, historical and religious heritage of the Kingdom of Cambodia. This sculpture is made of metal and measures 41.50 x 22.50 x 49 cm in height.
In 2014, authorities in Basel confiscated a statue of the Buddha, which is believed to be part of Cambodia's cultural heritage. Under Swiss law, confiscated cultural property is returned to the country of origin.
At the ceremony, the Ambassador warmly welcomed the return of the statue after being displaced for years due to a protracted civil war, smuggling and trafficking. Go abroad.
He added that "the pleasure of receiving the statue back to Cambodia is priceless."
The Ambassador also thanked the Swiss government, in particular the Office of Federal Culture and the Office of the Public Prosecutor of Basel, and other stakeholders for their significant legal and technical assistance and cooperation in preventing the escape. Taxes on our cultural artifacts, as well as facilitation of the return of antiquities.
The Ambassador stressed that Cambodia is conducting research to gather evidence of important cultural assets that have been illegally stolen from our nation through cooperation with the international community and stakeholders for reclaiming and monitoring processes. Effectively. In this spirit, he requested that all museums, institutions and curators of Khmer antiquities continue to volunteer to return our important national cultural artifacts to Cambodia.
The Royal Government of Cambodia considers "the repatriation of Cambodian artifacts as a noble and respectable gesture that reflects ethics and thoughtfulness in contributing to the nation's cultural values and is a noble effort to "Reconcile and heal the emotional wounds of Cambodians who have endured years of civil war."
Ms Fabienne Baraga also reaffirmed the Swiss government's commitment to curbing the illegal trade and trafficking of cultural assets and the preservation of human cultural heritage.
