According to the two media outlets, Teav Vannul claimed that the government had placed a camera in front of his house to monitor the activities 24 hours a day. Mr. Teav Vannul also said that the government led by His Majesty Hun Manet had worsened in terms of democracy, continuing to arrest political rivals and people who criticized the government.
A government spokesman has categorically denied Teav Vannul's claim and would like to inform the national and international public as follows:
First, there is no camera to monitor the activities of Mr. Teav Vannul, as claimed by his slander. Cameras placed in public places throughout the country, as well as in the geography of Phnom Penh, are for security, safety, public order and traffic only to the general public, and there is no policy to place separate cameras to monitor the activities of any individual. No.
Second, law enforcement is applied to those who have violated the law, regardless of their status or political status. In Cambodia, there are no political prisoners, only politicians who commit illegal acts and are punished by law.
Third, law enforcement is an important task necessary to strengthen the rule of law. In all countries, especially in developed democracies, strengthening the rule of law is essential for democracy to function and grow. There is no respect for the law, no democracy, but it is a source of anarchy, that is, democracy and individual rights must be under the law.
Fourth, Article 41 of the Constitution of the Kingdom of Cambodia states that the Cambodian people have the right to freedom of expression, freedom of the press, freedom of the press and freedom of assembly. No one shall be subjected to arbitrary interference with his privacy, family, home or correspondence, nor to attacks upon his honor and reputation. The press regime is regulated by law.
Fifth, in Cambodia, expressions within the framework of the Constitution, as mentioned earlier, and constructive criticism are firmly respected and guaranteed. But violations that are prohibited by the constitution are illegal and law enforcement is not interpreted as a suppression of freedom of expression or persecution.
A government spokesman said that Teav Vannul's remarks were intended to tarnish the government's image as the Senate election approaches. Only sympathy from the public. This is evidence that abusive speech is detrimental to the dignity of others, which is prohibited by the Constitution.
