Thailand, Sri Lanka sign free trade agreement
(Colombo): Thailand and Sri Lanka last weekend signed a free trade agreement that hopes to help Sri Lanka recover from its worst financial crisis in decades. According to the Bangkok Post.
The island nation is continuing to focus on trade deals to boost growth and help its struggling economy, which the World Bank estimated fell 3.8 percent last year after the foreign exchange crisis. Seriously, it has caused the financial crisis to intensify.
The Bangkok Post reported, citing a government statement, that the free trade agreement (FTA) aims to increase market opportunities by covering trade, investment, customs and intellectual property.
"This agreement will provide a lot of business opportunities for both sides," Thai Prime Minister Setha Thavisin told a joint news conference after the signing. "We encourage our private sector to explore the potential of bilateral trade and investment."
A Thai delegation led by Setha arrived in Colombo on Saturday to sign the free trade agreement, along with other agreements. The two countries also signed a new bilateral air service agreement.
The value of trade between Thailand and Sri Lanka from January to October last year totaled $ 320.4 million, according to the Trade Negotiation Department of the Thai Ministry of Commerce.
Thailand's main exports are rubber products, fabrics, gems, machinery and plastics, while imports from Sri Lanka include jewelry, electronics, clothing, plant products and chemicals. The new FTA is expected to benefit Thailand's exports of auto parts, textiles, electronics, machinery, metal products, paper, processed foods and animal feed. According to the Bangkok Post.
