(Battambang): Dr. Eang Sophalleth, Minister of Environment, identified three challenges that are devastating the Tonle Sap Lake. The third problem is forest fires, forest encroachment, land grabbing and climate change.
Dr. Eang Sophalleth said that the Tonle Sap Lake is Cambodia's main source of protein, which means it is home to hundreds of diverse fish species for the needs of millions of Cambodians every year.
Minister Eang Sophalleth said that the first challenge is the issue of wildfires, which are becoming a concern causing the destruction of the Tonle Sap Lake. As a matter of urgency before the Minister, the Minister advised to participate in preventing further wildfires, and at the same time he encouraged the replanting of more seedlings under the cooperation between the Ministry of Agriculture and the Ministry of Environment. Both communities.
At this point, Dr. Eang Sophalleth called on some individuals, especially the authorities, to immediately stop all deforestation activities to encroach on the land in the Tonle Sap Lake, considering the common interests, while the Tonle Sap Lake is A source of fish to feed millions of Cambodians.
The third challenge is climate change, with the minister emphasizing that it is difficult to prevent because climate change is a global problem. This has made the Tonle Sap Lake, the largest freshwater lake in Southeast Asia, the most vulnerable.
However, in the seventh mandate, as the Minister of Environment, Dr. Eang Sophalleth launched a new strategy, the Chakra Strategy for the environment, focusing on cleanliness, greenery and sustainability to combat climate change. .
