(Phnom Penh): Sing Bay Otec (Cambodia) is the first Cambodian company to invest in a plantation. The company was granted the right to use state forest land for industrial tree planting under the cooperation of the Forestry Administration of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries in 2011 on a total area of 34,007 hectares, which is the land of the Boeung Char Forest Promotion and Rehabilitation Station. In Kratie province and Siem Bok district, Stung Treng province.
Investing in plantations is more difficult and complicated than investing in other sectors. For Cambodia, Sing Bay Otec, with Cambodian investors in collaboration with foreigners, was the first company to break through the difficulties of establishing a plantation industry.
When asked why he was investing in plantations, a complex industry, Chea Pov, a CEO of Sing Biotech, highlighted that his investment in plantations was due to He is an expert in tree planting. In addition, he saw that Cambodia has a high potential for afforestation, especially that he intends to contribute to the re-establishment of Cambodia's lost forest cover.
The forest issue is a sensitive issue for Cambodian society, and the afforestation industry is a strange thing for Cambodian society. So many problems and obstacles become barriers to investing in plantations. Apart from the lack of experts, it is also difficult for the relevant officials as well as the people to have confidence in the company's investment.
In good faith, Cambodia wants to benefit economically and environmentally from the afforestation industry, Sing Bay Otec has overcome all obstacles and difficulties in cultivating this timber. Currently, about 15,000 hectares of land, equivalent to about 20 million saplings, have been planted by the company and some trees have started to be harvested.
Sing Bay Otec's investment in plantations not only contributes to the national economy and the creation of environmentally friendly forest cover, but also provides employment and income to people in remote areas. For people to travel and transport their crops as well.
Mr. Chhit Nonn, Chief of Kampong Cham Commune, Sambo District, Kratie Province, saw that Sing Biotech Company has a clear willingness to invest and has actually planted trees. In addition, the company's investment has provided two major benefits: first, the provision of employment and decent wages, and second, the company has built roads to facilitate travel and transportation of agricultural products, which were previously very difficult.
According to Mr. Chhit Non, the investment in Sing Bay Otec's plantation also contributes to the elimination of natural logging activities. In addition, after working in the plantation of Sing Bay Otec, some people gave up their logging business.
Investment and industrialization are important sources of income for people, and even more so for people in remote rural areas. Sing Bay Otec's investment in plantations has contributed to providing many jobs for people and helping them to have a clear source of income to support their livelihoods.
Ms. Loch Sarin, a resident of Kampong Cham Commune, Sambo District, Kratie Province, came to work at the plywood factory of Sing Biotech with her husband. She and her husband can earn more than 1 million riel per month.
Ms. Loch Sarin pointed out that in rural areas, finding a job is difficult. Therefore, Sing Bay Otec's investment gives her a regular income to solve problems in life and livelihood.
Based on his many years of study and involvement in forestry, Chea Pov said that Cambodia has high potential for investing in plantations. He said that currently Cambodia has an area of about 2 million hectares that can be invested in plantations in the northeast and southwest of the country.
For Mr. Chea Pov, with the support of the Royal Government and relevant institutions in Cambodia, it will take about 15 years to reap the benefits of this plantation. Mr. Chea Pov saw that if the investment in plantations is successful, it will contribute to providing employment to one million people and can provide economic benefits for the nation of not less than 5 billion dollars per year.
