The Ministry of Public Works in collaboration with the International Transport Forum (ITF) organizes a survey workshop with stakeholders on improving regional shipping connectivity in Southeast Asia
This workshop was held on February 1, 2024 under the chairmanship of Mr. Leng Thun Yuthea, Secretary of State, Representative of Mr. Peng Po Na, Minister of Public Works and Transport, and was conducted with the direct participation of relevant units in the Ministry of Inter-Ministerial Representation. About 40 representatives of the Cambodian Land Transport Association and representatives of the Cambodian Logistics Association, as well as the participation of ITF representatives through video.
The main objectives of this workshop are: Gather input and input from stakeholders, both public and private, in Cambodia and in the region related to policy frameworks, development plans, priority areas and challenges in the transport and logistics sector. Identify gaps and prepare recommendations within the regional framework to improve regional shipping connectivity in Southeast Asia.
Speaking on that occasion, Mr. Leng Thunyuthea highlighted the important achievements of the development of transport and logistics infrastructure in Cambodia under the skillful leadership of Mr. Peng Por Na, Minister of Public Works and Transport and the unique guide of Samdech Akka Moha Sena Padei Techo. Hun Sen, former Prime Minister of the Kingdom of Cambodia and the wisest leadership of His Majesty the King Hun Manet, Prime Minister of the Kingdom of Cambodia.
Key achievements include the development of expressways, the connection of national and provincial roads, the development of the waterway-port network, the development of the new Phnom Penh-Siem Reap International Airport, and the strengthening of bilateral and multilateral cooperation on transit transport. Borders, strengthening governance in the field of transport and logistics, and developing a comprehensive master plan on the Cambodian transport and logistics system 2023-2033, etc.
