The ADB has pledged $ 9.8 billion in climate financing from its own resources for 2023, of which $ 5.5 billion for impact mitigation work and $ 4.3 billion for adaptation work. This is an increase of more than 46% compared to the commitment to provide climate financing for 2022.
Adaptation financing is important in the Asia-Pacific region, which is experiencing extreme heat, drought and heavy rains, but investment in adaptation work in the region is still small compared to the need.
As the Climate Bank for Asia and the Pacific, ADB is committed to assisting our developing members in eliminating the use of fossil fuels for a progressive economy along a climate-interactive path and achieving a balance of greenhouse gas emissions. Of the country. We must work together urgently and responsibly to meet specific needs.
Asia and the Pacific account for more than half of the world's carbon emissions, while the region is particularly vulnerable to the effects of climate change. The region needs to invest about $ 3.1 trillion a year just for energy resources and transportation to achieve the balance of greenhouse gas emissions by 2050, which is more than enough. Currently about 50%.
As the Climate Bank of Asia and the Pacific, the ADB aims to provide $ 100 billion in climate financing from its own resources from 2019 to 2030.
In 2022, the ADB has committed $ 6.7 billion in climate financing from its own resources, including $ 4 billion for mitigation work and $ 2.7 billion for adaptation. Interior Ministry spokesman: 72 people arrested for criminal and drug offenses on 31 January
