EU and German ambassadors pledge to continue to assist Cambodia in Tonle Sap environmental conservation and biodiversity
Confirmation of the commitment of the ambassadors of the two superpowers was made at a press conference after visiting the Tonle Sap Lake on the afternoon of January 31, 2024 at Ramsar Prek Toal in Ek District. Battambang.
Igor Driesmans said that the European Union's participation in the conservation of the Tonle Sap ecosystem will benefit the people living in the area. This means that if Cambodia has a beautiful nature and environment, it will be able to attract tourists to visit Cambodia, eco-tourism, which becomes an important role to improve the lives of local people through various services.
He stressed that the potential of ecotourism is very important, but requires more active participation from all stakeholders.
The German ambassador said that Germany has provided substantial funding to improve the income generation of local communities in the Ramsar, Prek Toal and Stung Treng areas, which is important to protect the biodiversity and ecosystems along the Mekong River.
The German ambassador stressed that all cross-border water cooperation projects supported by the Mekong River Commission (MRC) are of imminent importance.
He said the MRC also supports cross-border projects with Thailand and Laos for better drought management, including components for monitoring plastic pollution in the Lower Mekong Basin. These activities were also supported by Germany.
Mr. Eang Sophalleth, Minister of Environment, thanked the Ambassadors of the European Union and Germany for their continued assistance to Cambodia on environmental and biodiversity issues, and in particular on the Tonle Sap Lake.
At the same time, Dr. Eang Sophalleth, Minister of Environment, also reaffirmed his commitment to prevent illegal encroachment on forest land within the jurisdiction of the Ministry of Environment.
